        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A dehumidifier that has stopped because its bucket is full has a capacity of zero pints. Whatever you paid for, whatever it says on the box, that is the number you are getting. Drainage is not an accessory to this purchase; it is the purchase.
        </p>

        <div id="three-routes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Three Routes, Compared Honestly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every dehumidifier condenses water into an internal pan. From there it goes to a removable bucket, or out of a drain port, or through a pump. Without a floor drain below the unit, you are choosing among three arrangements, and the right one depends almost entirely on whether there is anything in your basement that water can flow downhill into.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Route</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Reliability</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best For</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bucket only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Depends on you</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Small, barely damp spaces</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gravity to a sink or pit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$10-25 hose</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very high, nothing to fail</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Anywhere with a low destination</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Built-in pump</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$40-80 on the unit price</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Good, one failure point</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No low destination at all</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">External condensate pump</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$40-70</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Good, adds a device</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Retrofitting a unit you own</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Take gravity if you possibly can. It has no moving parts, no electricity, and nothing that can fail in a way that leaves the basement wet. A utility sink, a sump pit, a shower drain in a basement bathroom, or a washing machine standpipe are all valid destinations, and any of them within hose reach and below the unit's drain port turns this from a problem into a ten-dollar hose.
          </p>

          <p>
            The important measurement is not distance, it is height. The drain port on most units sits four to eight inches off the floor, which means a destination has to be lower than that, or the unit has to be raised until it is. Standing a dehumidifier on a concrete block or a sturdy crate is the single most common way people convert an impossible gravity run into a working one, and it costs nothing.
          </p>

          <p>
            If nothing in the basement is lower than the unit, you need a pump. That is not a failure, it is just the situation in a great many houses, and a condensate pump is a cheap, common, and well-understood device. The choice is then between one built into the dehumidifier and a separate one sitting beside it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gravity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Gravity Drainage: Five Rules That Decide Whether It Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gravity drainage fails in predictable ways, and all of them come down to the same physical requirement: water has to run downhill the entire way, without exception.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Continuous Fall, With No Dips</h3>

          <p>
            This is the rule people break. A hose that runs across the floor and sags into a low spot holds water in that sag. The trapped water blocks the line, condensate backs up into the unit's pan, the float switch reads full, and the machine stops. From the outside this looks like a broken dehumidifier. Route the hose so it falls continuously, and clip or tape it to the wall if the floor is uneven.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Remove the Bucket Float Correctly</h3>

          <p>
            Most units require something specific when you switch to continuous drainage: a knockout removed, a plug pulled, or the bucket left in place because the float still needs it. Check the manual for your model. A unit plumbed to a drain while its float still thinks the bucket is present will keep shutting off, and people spend a long time blaming the hose.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use the Shortest Run You Can</h3>

          <p>
            Every foot of hose is another foot where a kink, a sag, or a bit of slime can stop the flow. A six-foot run to a nearby sump pit is far more reliable than a thirty-foot run across the basement to a utility sink, even though both are technically downhill. If the choice is between a longer gravity run and a short pumped one, the short pumped one often wins.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do Not Submerge the Hose End</h3>

          <p>
            The discharge end should sit above the water line in a sump pit or sink, not below it. A submerged end can siphon back, and it will certainly grow a biofilm that works its way up the hose. Cut it to length so it discharges into air.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Secure Both Ends</h3>

          <p>
            A hose that works loose from a sink edge and falls onto the floor will empty several gallons a day onto the slab, and because a dehumidifier is quiet and unattended, you may not find out for a week. Zip-tie or clamp the discharge end to whatever it is draining into.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pumps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Internal Pump or External Pump?
          </h2>

          <p>
            If nothing is lower than the unit, a pump lifts the condensate up to a sink, a window, or a drain line above. Both types do the same job well; the difference is in how you buy them and how you live with them.
          </p>

          <p>
            A built-in pump is neater. It is integrated, there is one power cord, there is nothing extra standing on the floor, and the unit manages it automatically. Typical lift is 12 to 16 feet vertically, which is more than enough to reach a laundry sink or a window. The costs are that it usually adds meaningfully to the price of the machine, it is one more thing inside the unit that can fail, and when it does fail you generally cannot replace just the pump.
          </p>

          <p>
            An external condensate pump is the better answer for a unit you already own, and often the better answer generally. It is a small reservoir with a float switch and a pump, it sits beside or below the dehumidifier, the unit gravity-drains a few inches into it, and the pump lifts the water wherever you need it. They cost forty to seventy dollars, they are a standard HVAC part available anywhere, and if one fails you replace it for the same again without touching the dehumidifier.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a noise consideration that surprises people. A condensate pump is silent until it discharges, then runs briefly with a distinct buzz. It is not loud, but it is a change in sound rather than a constant hum, and that is exactly what people notice at night. With an external pump you can put the noisy part somewhere less disruptive; with an internal one it is wherever the dehumidifier is.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you choose, look for a pump with a safety switch, sometimes called an overflow or high-water cutout. It detects a rising reservoir the pump has failed to clear and shuts the dehumidifier off rather than letting the pump reservoir overflow. It costs a few dollars more and it is the difference between a dead pump and a wet basement floor.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE FAILURE MODE TO DESIGN AROUND</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A pump that fails silently empties onto your floor.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Gravity drainage fails safe: the machine stops. A pump fails wet unless it has a high-water safety switch wired to shut the dehumidifier down. If you are running a pump in a finished basement, that switch is not optional, and a battery water alarm on the floor beside it costs ten dollars.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="where-water-goes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Where the Water Can Sensibly Go
          </h2>

          <p>
            Condensate is close to distilled water, but it has passed over a coil and through a pan, so treat it as greywater rather than something clean. Here are the realistic destinations, in rough order of preference.
          </p>

          <p>
            A utility sink or laundry tub is the best target: it is designed for water, it is trapped and vented, and it is usually already there. Discharge above the rim, not into standing water, and secure the hose so it cannot fall out.
          </p>

          <p>
            A sump pit is excellent if you have one, and it is where most basement dehumidifiers with gravity drainage end up. The pump is already handling groundwater, and a few gallons a day is nothing to it. Two cautions: keep the hose end above the water line, and if your pit has a sealed radon lid, run the hose through a proper grommet rather than leaving the lid ajar, which defeats the sealing.
          </p>

          <p>
            A washing machine standpipe works and is a common choice for a pumped discharge, but check the arrangement. The standpipe is trapped, and a dehumidifier running in shoulder season may not send enough water to keep that trap wet, which lets sewer gas into the basement. If the dehumidifier is the only thing using it for weeks at a time, that is worth knowing about.
          </p>

          <p>
            Outdoors through a window or rim joist is workable in summer and a common approach with a pump, but it has to be undone or protected before the first freeze, because a hose full of water in a freezing wall penetration will burst. It is also worth not discharging it straight down against the foundation, which puts the water you just removed back into the soil beside your basement wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watering plants with it is fine in principle, and people do it, but note two things: the volume is large, a few gallons a day, and a pan that has not been cleaned can carry biofilm. It is not drinking water for pets, and it is not suitable for an aquarium or a humidifier without treatment. Where a condensate line may legally connect to household drainage varies by jurisdiction, so check local code before making a permanent connection.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hoses-freezing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Hose Choice, Freezing, and Backflow
          </h2>

          <p>
            The hardware is simple, which is exactly why people give it no thought and then have problems.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most units take a standard three-quarter inch garden hose thread on the drain port. The short length supplied in the box is almost always too short to be useful, and a proper garden hose is cheap. What matters more than the brand is the wall stiffness: a thin vinyl hose kinks at any bend and a kink stops the flow completely. A reinforced hose that holds its shape around a corner is worth the extra few dollars.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clear vinyl tubing has one real advantage: you can see the water moving and see a blockage forming. For a pumped discharge, which usually uses smaller three-eighths inch tubing, clear tubing makes diagnosing a slow line trivial. For a gravity run where the hose is out of sight along a wall, an opaque hose is fine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Freezing matters if any part of the run passes through an unheated space or out through a wall. Water left in a hose through a cold snap expands and splits it, and you find out in spring when the basement floor is wet. If you drain outdoors, either bring the line in for winter, or arrange it so it drains completely by gravity with no low point that can hold water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Backflow is worth one sentence of thought. If your discharge point can ever back up, a floor drain that surcharges in a storm, or a sink that gets blocked, water can travel back up an unrestricted hose toward the unit. Keeping the discharge end above the potential water level and above the unit's own pan level handles this, and it is another reason not to submerge the hose end.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Slime, Clogs, and the Twenty Minutes a Year This Needs
          </h2>

          <p>
            A condensate line is a permanently damp, dark, room-temperature tube, which is an excellent home for biofilm. This is the single most common cause of a drainage setup that worked beautifully for two seasons and then stopped.
          </p>

          <p>
            The symptom is usually mistaken for something else. The unit starts stopping intermittently with a full-bucket indication even though it is plumbed to a drain, or you find a small puddle under the machine. What has happened is that slime has narrowed the hose or the drain port until condensate arrives faster than it can leave, and the pan backs up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once a year, before the humid season, disconnect the hose and flush it. A few cups of warm water with a little white vinegar, run through and then flushed with clean water, deals with normal biofilm. For a stubborn line, a length of trimmer line or a plumber's zip strip pushed through will clear a physical blockage. While the hose is off, check the drain port on the unit itself, which is where the narrowing usually starts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean the intake filter at the same time, and more often during the season. In a basement it loads with dust much faster than the manual assumes. A clogged filter reduces airflow across the coil, which reduces capacity, lengthens runtime, and can push the coil cold enough to frost, which produces a machine that appears to be running and is producing almost no water.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you run a condensate pump, its reservoir needs the same attention. Most have a lid that lifts off; the float and the small reservoir accumulate the same biofilm, and a float that sticks either runs the pump dry or fails to start it. The same vinegar solution, and a wipe with a cloth, is all it needs. Twenty minutes once a year prevents almost every drainage failure people write in about.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Annual Drainage Service, By the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Time for the whole job</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$2</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cost in vinegar</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1x</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per year, before humid season</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Most</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Drainage failures it prevents</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="troubleshooting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When a Working Drain Suddenly Stops
          </h2>

          <p>
            The diagnostic order below moves from most to least common, and gets you to the cause quickly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Unit Keeps Stopping With a Full Indication</h3>

          <p>
            Nine times in ten this is a restricted line, not a sensor fault. Pull the hose off and see whether water runs freely from the port. If the port drips slowly, the blockage is in the unit. If the port runs freely, the blockage is in the hose or the hose has a sag holding water. Flush both.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Bucket Fills Even Though It Is Plumbed</h3>

          <p>
            This is almost always the continuous-drain conversion not being completed. Most units need a knockout removed, a rubber plug pulled, or a diverter moved, and they are not identical between models. Recheck the manual for the specific step your unit needs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Puddle Under the Unit, Hose Clear</h3>

          <p>
            Check that the machine is level in both directions. A unit tilted away from its drain port will fill the pan to the low side and spill before reaching the outlet. Also check the hose connection itself, which is a common slow leak, and confirm the washer is present in the fitting.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Pump Runs But Nothing Reaches the Destination</h3>

          <p>
            Either the lift exceeds the pump's rating, or the discharge tubing is kinked or blocked. Check the pump's rated lift against the actual vertical height, and remember that a long horizontal run adds resistance too. Clear vinyl tubing makes this trivial to diagnose.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Pump Does Not Run At All</h3>

          <p>
            Usually a stuck float in a reservoir that has grown biofilm. Lift the lid, clean the reservoir and the float, and check it moves freely. If the pump has a safety switch that has tripped, the dehumidifier will also be off, which is the switch doing its job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the Hose Across the Floor With a Sag in It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gravity needs continuous fall along the entire run. A hose that dips into a low spot holds water there, the line blocks, condensate backs up into the pan, and the float switch stops the machine. From the outside this looks like a failed dehumidifier. Clip the hose to the wall and raise the unit on a block to buy the fall.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Completing the Continuous-Drain Conversion</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most units need something specific done before the drain port works: a knockout removed, a plug pulled, or the bucket left in place for the float. A unit plumbed to a drain that still thinks its bucket is absent will keep shutting off, and people blame the hose for weeks. Check the manual for your model.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a Condensate Pump Without a Safety Switch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gravity drainage fails safe, because the machine simply stops. A pump fails wet. A high-water safety switch that shuts the dehumidifier down when the reservoir is not clearing costs a few dollars and is the difference between a dead pump and a flooded finished basement. Add a ten-dollar water alarm beside it too.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Submerging the Discharge End in the Sump or Sink</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A hose end below the water line can siphon back toward the unit, and it will certainly grow a biofilm that works its way up the line. Cut the hose so it discharges into air above the water level, and secure it so it cannot fall out and empty gallons a day onto the slab.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never Flushing the Line</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A condensate hose is a permanently damp, dark tube, and biofilm narrows it over two or three seasons until the pan backs up. This is the most common cause of a drainage setup that worked and then stopped. Twenty minutes once a year with warm water and white vinegar prevents nearly all of it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I drain a dehumidifier in a basement without a floor drain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Three options. Gravity-drain by hose to any destination lower than the unit's drain port, such as a utility sink or a sump pit, raising the unit on a block to get the fall. Buy a unit with a built-in condensate pump. Or add a separate external condensate pump, which costs forty to seventy dollars and works with any dehumidifier.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a dehumidifier drain into a sump pump pit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is one of the most common arrangements. The pump is already handling groundwater and a few gallons a day is nothing to it. Keep the hose end above the water line rather than submerged, and if the pit has a sealed radon lid, pass the hose through a proper grommet instead of leaving the lid ajar.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a condensate pump for my dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if nothing in the basement sits lower than the unit's drain port. Because that port is typically four to eight inches off the floor, standing the dehumidifier on a concrete block or crate frequently converts an impossible gravity run into a working one. Check the heights before buying a pump.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a built-in pump better than an external one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Built-in is neater, with one cord and nothing extra on the floor. External is better for a unit you already own, costs less than the price difference on a pumped model, is a standard part you can replace without touching the dehumidifier, and lets you put the pump noise somewhere less disruptive. Both lift roughly 12 to 16 feet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my dehumidifier say the bucket is full when it is plumbed to a drain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually a restricted line rather than a faulty sensor. Slime narrows the hose or the drain port until water arrives faster than it leaves and the pan backs up. Pull the hose and check whether the port runs freely. The other common cause is an incomplete continuous-drain conversion, where a knockout or plug was never removed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I drain a dehumidifier outside?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In summer, yes, and it is a common arrangement with a pump. Two cautions: bring the line in or drain it fully before the first freeze, because a hose full of water in a wall penetration will split; and do not discharge straight down against the foundation, which returns the water you just removed to the soil beside your basement wall.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is dehumidifier water safe to use on plants?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For ornamental plants, generally yes, and the volume makes it tempting at a few gallons a day. It is not drinking water for people or pets, it is not suitable for an aquarium or a humidifier, and it should not be used on edibles, because it has passed over a coil and through a pan that may carry biofilm and trace metals.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

