        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Try gravity first, and try harder than seems necessary. The drain port on most units is only four to eight inches off the floor, so a concrete block under the machine converts a great many impossible runs into working ones. A utility sink, a sump pit, a shower drain or a standpipe are all valid destinations, and a ten-dollar hose with continuous fall and no sag is the most reliable arrangement available.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If nothing is lower than the unit, use a pump, and insist on a high-water safety switch that shuts the dehumidifier off when the reservoir is not clearing. Gravity fails safe and a pump fails wet. Then flush the line once a year with warm water and vinegar, which takes twenty minutes and prevents most of the drainage failures that make people think their dehumidifier has died.
          </p>
